WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.166 --> 00:00:01.901
The security situation playing out

00:00:01.901 --> 00:00:03.737
before the former president

00:00:03.737 --> 00:00:05.238
has turned himself in

00:00:05.238 --> 00:00:06.873
on those charges in Georgia.

00:00:06.873 --> 00:00:08.108
Sources tell us that

00:00:08.108 --> 00:00:10.076
most likely to happen next week

00:00:10.076 --> 00:00:11.411
as his legal team is working out

00:00:11.411 --> 00:00:13.680
the details of his surrender

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.682
at the Fulton County Jail.

00:00:15.682 --> 00:00:16.683
Let's discuss this

00:00:16.683 --> 00:00:17.450
case, broader

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:18.952
implications with former Trump

00:00:18.952 --> 00:00:19.419
White House

00:00:19.419 --> 00:00:20.820
Press Secretary Stephanie Grisham

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:22.722
and Republican election lawyer

00:00:22.722 --> 00:00:23.656
Ben Ginsberg.

00:00:23.656 --> 00:00:25.058
Good to have you both on today.

00:00:25.058 --> 00:00:26.226
Thanks so much.

00:00:26.226 --> 00:00:27.894
Stephanie, I want to ask you this.

00:00:27.894 --> 00:00:30.063
You look at the doxing of these jurors

00:00:30.063 --> 00:00:31.931
you look at these threats, for instance,

00:00:31.931 --> 00:00:33.666
to get against the judge here.

00:00:33.666 --> 00:00:36.002
The former president has attacked

00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:36.770
the system.

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:38.171
He's attacked the judges.

00:00:38.171 --> 00:00:40.106
He's made personal and public attacks

00:00:40.106 --> 00:00:41.107
against some of the witnesses

00:00:41.107 --> 00:00:41.708
in this case.

00:00:41.708 --> 00:00:44.911
Has he helped to give license by his own

00:00:44.911 --> 00:00:46.446
public attacks

00:00:46.446 --> 00:00:47.447
to this kind of behavior

00:00:47.447 --> 00:00:48.448
in these kinds of threats?

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:51.918
Well, absolutely.

00:00:51.918 --> 00:00:52.352
I mean, and

00:00:52.352 --> 00:00:54.020
you can look to January six,

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:56.689
I think, as a huge, huge example.

00:00:56.689 --> 00:00:57.457
But, you know,

00:00:57.457 --> 00:00:58.792
what gets me is that I know

00:00:58.792 --> 00:00:59.459
very few

00:00:59.459 --> 00:01:01.628
Republicans want to upset Donald Trump

00:01:01.628 --> 00:01:03.696
in any way right now.

00:01:03.696 --> 00:01:04.631
But I'm asking,

00:01:04.631 --> 00:01:06.299
why are there no Republicans,

00:01:06.299 --> 00:01:07.100
most specifically

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:08.401
the people running against him

00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:10.070
for the Republican nomination,

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:11.671
calling on him

00:01:11.671 --> 00:01:13.473
to put something out, to say

00:01:13.473 --> 00:01:14.974
there should be no violence,

00:01:14.974 --> 00:01:15.975
please do not threaten

00:01:15.975 --> 00:01:16.910
anybody having to do

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:19.179
with my criminal many criminal cases

00:01:19.179 --> 00:01:20.713
Please do not threaten the jurors.

00:01:20.713 --> 00:01:21.514
How is it

00:01:21.514 --> 00:01:22.315
that there's absolutely

00:01:22.315 --> 00:01:23.683
not any Republicans out there

00:01:23.683 --> 00:01:25.051
that I'm aware of that

00:01:25.051 --> 00:01:26.186
are calling on Trump?

00:01:26.186 --> 00:01:27.854
All of them should be calling on Trump

00:01:27.854 --> 00:01:28.955
to say something

00:01:28.955 --> 00:01:30.090
because I think that will also

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:30.824
show voters

00:01:30.824 --> 00:01:31.891
who he really is, that

00:01:31.891 --> 00:01:33.159
he won't keep quiet

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:34.561
or he won't say anything.

00:01:34.561 --> 00:01:35.662
He'll stay quiet

00:01:35.662 --> 00:01:36.496
and allow people

00:01:36.496 --> 00:01:38.198
to continue to be threatened.

00:01:38.198 --> 00:01:38.998
Yeah, it's a good point

00:01:38.998 --> 00:01:39.666
because it's exactly

00:01:39.666 --> 00:01:40.800
what folks were asking for

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:41.768
on January six.

00:01:41.768 --> 00:01:42.902
Right, for a public statement

00:01:42.902 --> 00:01:45.105
to call back to the rioters.

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:47.574
Ben, I think many people were surprised

00:01:47.574 --> 00:01:48.274
to learn

00:01:48.274 --> 00:01:50.009
that under Georgia law,

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:51.911
grand jurors identities,

00:01:51.911 --> 00:01:52.879
they are released,

00:01:52.879 --> 00:01:55.014
listed in public court documents.

00:01:55.048 --> 00:01:56.549
That's the way it is.

00:01:56.583 --> 00:01:59.185
I wonder if that law survives

00:01:59.185 --> 00:02:00.587
the current time we're in.

00:02:00.587 --> 00:02:02.422
Is that, from your perspective,

00:02:02.422 --> 00:02:04.057
someone who's participated in many cases

00:02:04.057 --> 00:02:05.859
and you're well aware of the politics

00:02:05.859 --> 00:02:07.660
around our systems today

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:09.362
and the public threats to them.

00:02:09.362 --> 00:02:10.196
So that change

00:02:12.265 --> 00:02:12.932
Well, look,

00:02:12.932 --> 00:02:15.468
the incentives behind releasing the names

00:02:16.069 --> 00:02:17.203
was a good one.

00:02:17.203 --> 00:02:18.271
It's because you do

00:02:18.271 --> 00:02:19.639
want some transparency

00:02:19.639 --> 00:02:20.673
in your government.

00:02:20.673 --> 00:02:22.142
But it was also ruled

00:02:22.142 --> 00:02:22.942
it was put in

00:02:22.942 --> 00:02:25.311
in a very different time from now.

00:02:26.079 --> 00:02:26.913
And so

00:02:26.913 --> 00:02:27.714
as you see,

00:02:27.714 --> 00:02:28.815
these grand jurors

00:02:28.815 --> 00:02:30.817
who did do their civic duty

00:02:30.817 --> 00:02:34.120
now come under harassment and attack,

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:35.855
there will be a rethinking

00:02:35.855 --> 00:02:37.190
of all these laws.

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:39.859
So it'll be a little bit of transparency

00:02:40.126 --> 00:02:42.428
in our government that gets taken away.

00:02:42.428 --> 00:02:45.965
Another institutional sort of denigrated.

00:02:46.533 --> 00:02:46.933
Yeah.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:48.635
And it's it was there for a reason.

00:02:48.635 --> 00:02:48.935
Right.

00:02:48.935 --> 00:02:51.171
But but the threats might very well

00:02:51.171 --> 00:02:51.971
outweigh it.

00:02:51.971 --> 00:02:53.373
But, Ben, another question for you

00:02:53.373 --> 00:02:54.474
before I go back to Steph.

00:02:54.474 --> 00:02:55.308
Stephanie,

00:02:55.475 --> 00:02:55.975
you've heard

00:02:55.975 --> 00:02:57.177
a lot of this whataboutism,

00:02:57.177 --> 00:02:57.443
which is,

00:02:57.443 --> 00:02:59.479
of course, a new national sport,

00:02:59.479 --> 00:03:00.413
but some people are now

00:03:00.413 --> 00:03:01.514
making comparisons

00:03:01.514 --> 00:03:02.715
between Trump's attempts

00:03:02.715 --> 00:03:03.783
to overturn the election

00:03:03.783 --> 00:03:04.517
and the many lies

00:03:04.517 --> 00:03:06.019
he shared about false

00:03:06.019 --> 00:03:08.655
election fraud and Al Gore's

00:03:09.489 --> 00:03:10.723
election disputes.

00:03:10.723 --> 00:03:11.391
In 2000

00:03:11.391 --> 00:03:13.393
of course, you took part in that

00:03:13.393 --> 00:03:15.061
on the Republican side,

00:03:15.061 --> 00:03:15.595
as the court

00:03:15.595 --> 00:03:17.263
process played out, of course, Al Gore

00:03:17.263 --> 00:03:18.831
eventually conceded the election.

00:03:18.831 --> 00:03:20.366
Do you consider

00:03:20.366 --> 00:03:22.368
those two events at all equivalent

00:03:24.871 --> 00:03:26.372
No, not in the least.

00:03:26.372 --> 00:03:29.242
Look, the Florida election that Al Gore

00:03:29.242 --> 00:03:31.945
contested was 537 votes

00:03:32.612 --> 00:03:34.447
under any semblance

00:03:34.447 --> 00:03:37.517
of what sort of proper behavior is.

00:03:37.717 --> 00:03:38.785
That's an election

00:03:38.785 --> 00:03:41.187
that you can contest in recount.

00:03:41.688 --> 00:03:44.457
Donald Trump went ahead and contested it

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:45.992
and asked for a recount

00:03:45.992 --> 00:03:47.594
and brought litigation,

00:03:47.594 --> 00:03:51.030
and he lost every one of those 63 cases.

00:03:51.464 --> 00:03:54.901
And the bargain in in that situation

00:03:54.901 --> 00:03:56.736
is that you can take the cases to court.

00:03:56.736 --> 00:03:58.004
You have every right.

00:03:58.004 --> 00:03:59.005
But in going to court,

00:03:59.005 --> 00:04:00.840
you also have to abide

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:03.076
by the decisions of the court.

00:04:03.076 --> 00:04:05.345
Donald Trump failed to do that.

00:04:05.345 --> 00:04:08.514
Al Gore conceded the election rightly

00:04:09.182 --> 00:04:10.183
and properly.

00:04:10.183 --> 00:04:12.285
And Donald Trump has continued

00:04:12.285 --> 00:04:13.753
to basically tear down

00:04:13.753 --> 00:04:14.621
the institution

00:04:14.621 --> 00:04:16.189
of American elections

00:04:16.189 --> 00:04:18.925
with unfounded charges.

00:04:18.925 --> 00:04:21.160
Stephanie, advisers do not believe

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:23.363
Trump is planning to take part in next

00:04:23.463 --> 00:04:24.063
next week's

00:04:24.063 --> 00:04:27.567
presidential GOP presidential debate.

00:04:28.001 --> 00:04:30.603
What do the other GOP candidates

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:33.606
need to do there to stand out

00:04:33.606 --> 00:04:36.342
and make themselves a viable alternative

00:04:36.509 --> 00:04:37.010
to Trump?

00:04:39.646 --> 00:04:40.313
Well, I would

00:04:40.313 --> 00:04:41.914
say respectfully,

00:04:41.914 --> 00:04:44.017
they need to stop kissing up

00:04:44.017 --> 00:04:44.717
to Donald Trump.

00:04:44.717 --> 00:04:46.586
That is clearly not working for them.

00:04:46.586 --> 00:04:47.920
And, you know, the base,

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:48.421
which is who

00:04:48.421 --> 00:04:50.123
they're all trying to appeal to right now

00:04:50.123 --> 00:04:50.456
in order

00:04:50.456 --> 00:04:52.158
to become the Republican nominee.

00:04:52.158 --> 00:04:54.227
They like people who are tough,

00:04:54.227 --> 00:04:55.862
clearly look at who they're

00:04:55.862 --> 00:04:56.496
they're going for

00:04:56.496 --> 00:04:57.764
right now in Donald Trump.

00:04:57.764 --> 00:04:59.499
But how are people supposed to think

00:04:59.499 --> 00:05:00.900
that these people,

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:02.568
these nominees are going to stand up

00:05:02.568 --> 00:05:03.002
to somebody

00:05:03.002 --> 00:05:03.670
like Putin

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:04.070
or Kim

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:04.737
Jong un

00:05:04.737 --> 00:05:05.705
or even some leaders

00:05:05.705 --> 00:05:07.206
of our allied countries

00:05:07.206 --> 00:05:08.441
or members of Congress

00:05:08.441 --> 00:05:10.310
if they won't stand up

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:11.944
to this one middle

00:05:11.944 --> 00:05:13.846
and actually older man.

00:05:13.846 --> 00:05:14.914
So I think that's something

00:05:14.914 --> 00:05:16.482
they need to do. They need to change.

00:05:16.482 --> 00:05:17.350
They need to pivot.

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:18.217
They need to just

00:05:18.217 --> 00:05:19.919
not only talk about their policies,

00:05:19.919 --> 00:05:22.021
they need to call him out and show

00:05:22.288 --> 00:05:24.691
that they have the the backbone.

00:05:24.691 --> 00:05:25.858
I'll just say that to do it.

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:29.028
We'll be watching for that backbone.

00:05:29.028 --> 00:05:30.363
Stephanie Grisham.

00:05:30.363 --> 00:05:31.064
Ben Ginsberg,

00:05:31.064 --> 00:05:32.131
thanks so much to both of you.

